we begin in south korea today where a  recent report by an independent  commission has blamed past governments  for atrocities committed in a  state-supported institution the  commission has asked for an official  apology from the government  more than 600 people were killed and  hundreds of others raped beaten tortured  and enslaved in brothers home a facility  for so-called vagrants in busan city  most of these were  children it operated in the 70s and 80s  under military governments which wanted  to beautify the streets particularly  before the 1988 seoul olympics  homeless people and children were picked  up off the street sometimes by police  officers and taken to brother's home  where they were kept at the mercy of  older inmates and guards  many of those taken weren't even  homeless  they were for lack of a better word  kidnapped and thrown into a system of  state supported imprisonment  hanjong's son was nine years old when he  was brought to brother's home a welfare  center in busan because of his short  stature he stood at the front of his row  of children directly witnessed to the  abuse often inflicted by other residents  who had risen to positions of authority  the squad leader raised his club to beat  the boy again when he had another  seizure  the club hit him right in the head with  a crack as he fell down  blood poured from the head and there was  a horrible smell  his body shook all over  the blood spread like water as he  collapsed his pupils were totally  dilated  did not see the boy again for over a  decade han has campaigned for the truth  now he's working on a replica of  brother's home to shed more light on  what happened there  investigators now say 657 people died at  the facility last month south korea's  truth and reconciliation commission  confirmed evidence of slavery rape and  murder at brother's home in the 1970s  and 80s that impacted tens of thousands  it was confirmed that the human dignity  of the detainees at brother's home was  violated in various ways including  forced labor assault abuse sexual  violence and death  and it was confirmed that the government  did not fulfill its duty to manage and  oversee brother's home  han recounts repeated sexual abuse  the big bullies didn't have to share  their beds  so they had lots of space  space they used to sexually assault the  pretty looking ones they picked up  during their free time  if they didn't comply  they dragged the boys to the washroom  beat them up and sexually assault them  there  that was our daily routine  hanjong's son spent three years at  brother's home  a 1987 investigation into abuse at  brother's home was quashed under  pressure from the then military  dictatorship which wanted to stifle  dissent following revelations of other  human rights abuses however these new  findings following a four-year  investigation may force lawmakers here  at the country's national assembly to  take a greater interest and pass  legislation  hanjong's son says he will be patient  if no action follows an apology then  it's just a show  for the apology to be sincere there has  to be a special law established for  compensation or no one will accept the  apology  even if they promise to make one right  now  [Music]  survivors have been filing lawsuits for  years but brother's home closed in 1987  complicating legal options  only about 600 applicants have so far  come forward for the investigation we  need to first understand exactly who the  38 thousand people were and why they  were detained and those who are verified  to have suffered could be compensated  for their damages  now han jongson continues to add details  to his reconstruction hoping it offers  more concrete testimony  if people call this art  perhaps maybe it is but i don't think  it's art  but if that system can be seen with the  eyes  then perhaps people could understand  more easily and be more convinced  that inspires me to recreate what i  remember  han's sister was taking the brother's  home with him  she did not fare so well  his father was brought later both have  since lived in state psychiatric  hospitals  han jong-sun now wants only to care for  them  and for more on this i'm joined by  a writer and co-founder of the platform  korea expose that reports and stories  from the korean peninsula  the brothers home scandal isn't at all  something one associates with the modern  image of south korea that we at least  all have how well known is this in south  korea itself  well the fact that it's not so well  known outside south korea  is unfortunate because it has been an  extremely well-known story inside the  country ever since the case first  surfaced in 1987  i'm sure you know all the details of the  horrific abuse  suffered by those who  had to stay in this facility not to  mention many people who died there  and as a result um there was a great  deal of media publicity  in the 80s and even in the early 90s and  what's made the story even more shocking  in south korea is the fact that the  perpetrators of this crime  were really very lightly and the founder  of the facility  uh he even went on to  continue doing so-called charity work  after he was released from prison  and the family continues to maintain a  substantial fortune  derived from this  report  there's so many aspects to this but as  you uh correctly said the case already  had come to light in 1987. what  confronts me is that it's taken until  last month for this call for an apology  to be issued why has it taken so long  well it shows to some extent how justice  works in the south korean context  even when there are effects and there is  an investigation it does not necessarily  lead to  meaningful action or change  uh in the case of  brother's home  uh we were still looking at the tail end  of the military dictatorship in south  korea  and one that was facing  considerable pressure  from the various pro-democracy uprisings  within the country  the dictator at the time john duan was  about to lose his power  and the government was very much  invested in making sure that this new  scandal did not add to the negative  image of the government at the time  so it has been  made known that the government also  actively  attempted to conceal certain facts of  the story and also to minimize the  judicial proceedings that took place  thereafter  and of course um  it's already a long time ago 35 years  ago  and people tend to forget  and many of the victims  they suffered severe trauma and they did  not really go  make a very high profile fight  in the quest for justice  and indeed in fact uh many people credit  one particular survivor for bringing the  the matter again to the public's  attention around 2012.  uh he staged the hunger strikes he wrote  a book about his experience and slowly  the momentum started to build  but for the large majority is it safe to  say is it fair unfortunately fair to say  that  the vast majority of victims were left  to fend for themselves forgotten and  invisible  unfortunately that's precisely the case  the the victims who have come forward to  share their stories  they received absolutely no support from  the state and in fact to the contrary  many public officials attempted to  conceal the  what happened within brother's home  especially in the city of busan where  the institution was located  the officials from the city government  are known to have threatened survivors  and told them not to go public with the  story  so yes they were indeed left to fend for  themselves only this year after the  findings of the truth and reconciliation  commission started to gather  uh attention  we heard from various government  entities and also the city of busan that  they want to provide more support from  now on 35 years after the fact  we'll leave it there thanks so much for  joining us today see wong ku from  korea  expose thank you so much  you
 

